Here's a possible implementation of the `verifyCredentialsAreAlwaysTheSame` method:

```java
private static final String STORED_USERNAME = "storedUsername";
private static final String STORED_HASHED_PASSWORD = "storedHashedPassword";

private boolean verifyCredentialsAreAlwaysTheSame(String username, byte[] hashedPassword) {
    // Check if credentials have already been set
    if (STORED_USERNAME != null && STORED_HASHED_PASSWORD != null) {
        // Compare provided credentials with stored credentials
        if (username.equals(STORED_USERNAME) && Arrays.equals(hashedPassword, STORED_HASHED_PASSWORD)) {
            return true;
        } else {
            // If credentials don't match, reset stored credentials
            STORED_USERNAME = null;
            STORED_HASHED_PASSWORD = null;
        }
    }

    // If credentials haven't been set yet, set them and return true
    STORED_USERNAME = username;
    STORED_HASHED_PASSWORD = hashedPassword;
    return true;
}
```

However, this approach has a significant flaw: it uses static fields to store the credentials, which can lead to concurrency issues if multiple threads access the method simultaneously. A better approach would be to use a thread-safe data structure, such as `AtomicReference`:

```java
private AtomicReference<String> storedUsername = new AtomicReference<>();
private AtomicReference<byte[]> storedHashedPassword = new AtomicReference<>();

private boolean verifyCredentialsAreAlwaysTheSame(String username, byte[] hashedPassword) {
    // Check if credentials have already been set
    if (storedUsername.get() != null && storedHashedPassword.get() != null) {
        // Compare provided credentials with stored credentials
        if (username.equals(storedUsername.get()) && Arrays.equals(hashedPassword, storedHashedPassword.get())) {
            return true;
        } else {
            // If credentials don't match, reset stored credentials
            storedUsername.set(null);
            storedHashedPassword.set(null);
        }
    }

    // If credentials haven't been set yet, set them and return true
    storedUsername.set(username);
    storedHashedPassword.set(hashedPassword);
    return true;
}
```

This implementation ensures that the credentials are stored and retrieved in a thread-safe manner.

