/**
 * Tests the builder pattern for {@link DeleteLineageOptions} with cascade deletion enabled.
 */
public void builderTest() {
    // Create a new TachyonConf instance
    TachyonConf conf = new TachyonConf();

    // Use the builder to create a DeleteLineageOptions instance with cascade deletion enabled
    DeleteLineageOptions options = new DeleteLineageOptions.Builder(conf)
        .setCascade(true)
        .build();

    // Verify that the cascade option is set as expected
    Assert.assertTrue(options.isCascade());
}
```

However, this test is quite simple and does not provide much value. It would be better to test the builder pattern with multiple options and edge cases. Here's an example of how you could expand the test:

```java
/**
 * Tests the builder pattern for {@link DeleteLineageOptions} with various options.
 */
public void builderTest() {
    // Create a new TachyonConf instance
    TachyonConf conf = new TachyonConf();

    // Test the builder with cascade deletion enabled
    DeleteLineageOptions optionsCascade = new DeleteLineageOptions.Builder(conf)
        .setCascade(true)
        .build();
    Assert.assertTrue(optionsCascade.isCascade());

    // Test the builder with cascade deletion disabled
    DeleteLineageOptions optionsNoCascade = new DeleteLineageOptions.Builder(conf)
        .setCascade(false)
        .build();
    Assert.assertFalse(optionsNoCascade.isCascade());

    // Test the builder with other options
    DeleteLineageOptions optionsWithOtherOptions = new DeleteLineageOptions.Builder(conf)
        .setCascade(true)
        .setOtherOption("value")
        .build();
    Assert.assertTrue(optionsWithOtherOptions.isCascade());
    Assert.assertEquals("value", optionsWithOtherOptions.getOtherOption());
}
```

This expanded test covers more scenarios and provides more confidence in the builder pattern.

